[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Grammarly costs over $140 a year, and according to its own documentation, everything you type is shipped to their servers and stored there. A more blunt name for that would be a key logger you pay for. This is Harper. It\'s a grammar checker that\'s free, written in Rust,'

[00:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and works with your Wi-Fi turned off. Before you get too excited, I typed, "What\'s going on? I\'m josh into it," and it didn\'t blink. Stay with me because both halves of that are the point. Now, with Harper, the more interesting'

[00:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'thing about it is what\'s missing. There\'s no AI in it at all. It\'s a rule-based engine. Your text gets tagged word by word, then ran through about 287 handwritten Rust rules. Rules are deterministic. The same sentence gets'

[00:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the same verdict every single time. Now, compare that to what Grammarly users keep complaining about. It tells you to delete a comment at 9 and then put it back at 10. That\'s a machine learning model changing its mind about your'

[00:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'writing. Harper never changes its mind. And because nothing ever leaves your machine, it\'s instant. The claim is under 10 milliseconds per check. One more thing before I live run this. Since November 2024, Harper belongs to'

[01:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Automatic, the WordPress company, which hired its creator and kept everything Apache 2.0. Hold that thought cuz I\'m going to circle back to it in just a second. If you enjoy coding tools to speed up your workflow, be sure to'

[01:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'subscribe. We have videos coming out all the time. Now, check this out. Brew install Harper or oneclick the VS Code extension. It\'s native to Apple Silicon. And now you can switch the Wi-Fi off. You can go fully offline. If you run'

[01:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this, I\'m going to type I ate and pineapple. Flagged instantly. If I type along the lines of I needthe the from the store, the \'the\' is flagged. I spell a word wrong now like your point is'

[01:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mute. It suggests an alternative. That correlation is literally a handwritten rule in the source code. I checked. I don\'t think it\'s too late. Spelled some things wrong there. Bad punctuation.'

[02:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Three underlines before I could even finish the sentence. No spinner, no cloud. There\'s nothing to wait for. And here\'s the part that\'s going to help us in development. I\'ll open a TypeScript file and write a comment. I\'m going to'

[02:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'write receive the payload. Again, I spelled some things wrong. Harper flags both typos inside the comment. It ignores every line of actual code around it. It does this across basically every'

[02:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'language we can write in. Harper ships a zero language server, so it drops into Neo, Vim, Zed, Helix, Emacs, one line of config. The Obsidian plugin, 200,000 downloads, checks your notes and skips'

[02:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'code fences, the exact thing Grammarly actually trips over. The whole engine compiles to web suddenly. So, writewithharper.com runs the full checker inside your'

[02:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'browser tab. That\'s your zeroinstalled test drive. Think of it this way. Grammarly is an app that watches you type. Harper is a component you install into your tool chain. Now, how does all this land? Grammarly\'s ML does things'

[03:07] [scene:talk]
